    <section class="block" id="faq">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="head reveal">
          <span class="eyebrow"><Icon name="MessageCircleQuestion" size={14} /> FAQ</span>
          <h2>Questions, answered.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="faq reveal">
          <details open>
            <summary>Is Larvyn free? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Yes, and it will stay free. There is no paid tier and no account to create: install it and open a project.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Do I need Laravel Herd or Flock? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>No. Larvyn uses whatever PHP, Composer and Node are on your PATH, and picks up Herd automatically when it is installed, including its site URLs. With <a href="/blog/flock-integration/">Flock</a> installed, it can also serve each app at its own .test domain with HTTPS, and every agent’s worktree at its own URL. If Composer is missing, Larvyn can install it for you and verifies the installer’s signature.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Which coding agents work with it? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity CLI, Cursor Agent, OpenCode, Aider and Pi. Larvyn detects which ones are installed and runs them in real terminals, so they behave exactly as they do in your shell.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Will agents touch my working copy? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Only if you want them to. With worktrees on, each agent gets its own <code>larvyn/&lt;task&gt;</code> branch in a separate folder, with your <code>.env</code> and SQLite database copied. Those copies are never committed. You review the diff and decide what to merge.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Does Larvyn change my project? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>No. It reads your project to show routes, models, logs and data, and only writes when you ask: saving the <code>.env</code> (a backup is kept first), editing a row, running a command or merging a branch.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>What happens when I quit Larvyn? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Your workspace comes back when you open it again: terminal tabs and splits with the last part of their output, and the dev services that were running. Claude Code and Codex resume the same conversation, and OpenCode, Antigravity, Pi and Aider continue the latest one. Turn it off in <b>Settings → Workbench → Restore on start</b>.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I update Larvyn? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Larvyn checks for new releases when it starts and every few hours. When one is out, <b>Update now</b> downloads it, verifies the checksum and restarts Larvyn. You can also check by hand in <b>Settings → About &amp; feedback</b>.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Can I extend Larvyn? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Yes, with plugins. A plugin can add commands to Ctrl K, services to the Stack and views to the sidebar. Open <b>Settings → Plugins → Marketplace</b> to install one, or browse them at <a href="/plugins/">larvyn.dev/plugins</a>. To write your own, start from the <a href={STARTER}>plugin starter</a>.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Are plugins safe to install? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Before installing, Larvyn shows what a plugin may do, with the sensitive permissions in red. Larvyn enforces those permissions itself, shows you each command before it first runs, and keeps an update that wants a new permission off until you approve it. Views can’t reach your files or the internet directly. If the marketplace revokes a harmful plugin, Larvyn turns it off.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Where is my data stored? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>On your machine, in a single state file in your app data folder. There are no accounts and nothing is sent anywhere.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Which systems does it run on? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>Windows 10 and 11, macOS 12 or newer (Apple Silicon and Intel) and Linux x86-64. Larvyn is in public beta on all three, so expect rough edges and tell us what breaks.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>Why install from the terminal? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>The beta builds are not code-signed yet, so a file downloaded in the browser shows a security warning. The install command downloads the same release from GitHub, verifies its checksum and puts it in your user folder without that warning. You can read the scripts first: <a href={`${SCRIPTS}/install.sh`}><code>install.sh</code></a> and <a href={`${SCRIPTS}/install.ps1`}><code>install.ps1</code></a>.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>How do I uninstall it? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>On macOS and Linux run <code>curl -fsSL {SCRIPTS}/install.sh | sh -s -- --uninstall</code>. On Windows run <code>$env:LARVYN_UNINSTALL = "1"; irm {SCRIPTS}/install.ps1 | iex</code>. Your settings stay in your config folder until you delete it.</p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary>I found a bug. Where do I report it? <Icon name="Plus" size={20} /></summary>
            <p>In the app, open <b>Settings → Report a bug</b>: it opens a GitHub issue with your Larvyn version and system already filled in. You can also <a href={ISSUES}>open an issue</a> directly, or ask questions and share ideas in <a href={DISCUSSIONS}>Discussions</a>. Security problems go through a <a href={`https://github.com/${RELEASES_REPO}/security/advisories/new`}>private report</a>, never a public issue.</p>
          </details>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

